@c CHAPTER: Usage
@node Usage
@chapter Usage

Guile Documentá comes with a program called @command{documenta}, which
you can use to generate API documentation for your Guile Scheme project.
The resulting documentation will be written in
@url{https://www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/, Texinfo} format.

For example, let's suppose we have a project called @sc{Bogus X} and
that we keep its source files in a folder as follows:

@example
bogus-x                ← Project directory
├── bogusx             ← Guile Scheme "package"
│   ├── module-a.scm   ← Guile Scheme module
│   └── module-b.scm
├── doc                ← Project documentation in Texinfo format
│   └── main.texi
├── tests
└── README.org
@end example


In the example, the project already has some documentation written by
hand in @file{doc/main.texi}, and we want to complement it with API
documentation. To do so, we generate it as follows:

@example
$ cd bogus-x
$ documenta api bogusx
@end example


The @ref{cli-api, api} subcommand will inspect all the modules in the
@file{bogusx} directory and build API reference documentation that
will then be saved in Texinfo format to the @file{doc/api} folder:

@example
bogus-x
├── bogusx
├── doc
│   ├── api             ← Newly extracted API documentation
│   │   └── index.texi
│   └── main.texi
├── tests
└── README.org
@end example


For more complex projects, you can pass a quoted list of the module
files and directories that compose the whole API of the project. For
example:

@example
$ cd another-project
$ documenta api "mylib.scm mylib"
@end example


Finally, we can integrate the API documentation into the
@file{main.texi} documentation using Texinfo's @code{@@include}
command. For example:

@verbatim
@include api/index.texi
@end verbatim
@c END CHAPTER: Usage



@c CHAPTER: CLI
@node CLI
@chapter CLI

Guile Documentá comes with the @command{documenta} program, which
recognizes the following subcommands:

@quotation Note
Guile Documentá does not currently extract @abbr{CLI, Command Line
Interface} documentation from the source code, but it is intended to
do so in the future. The following documentation is (re)written by
hand (sadly).
@end quotation


@anchor{cli-api}
@section @code{api} command

The @code{api} command extracts @abbr{API, Application Programming
Interface} documentation from one or more Guile Scheme modules.

@subsection Examples

@example
# Extract API documentation from one Scheme file:
documenta api mylib.scm

# Extract API documentation from one Scheme directory (recursively):
documenta api mylib

# Extract API documentation from both a Scheme file and directory:
documenta api "mylib.scm mylib"
@end example

@quotation Note
Having to quote the list of sources in the last example is a limitation
that is expected to be removed in the future. So it could simply become:

@example
documenta api mylib.scm mylib etc
@end example
@end quotation

@example
# Save API documentation to a specific directory:
documenta api mylib --destination mydoc/api
@end example

@subsection Interface specification

@verbatim
documenta api sources [--destination]
@end verbatim

@defblock
@defline Argument sources
Absolute or relative paths to Guile Scheme modules that compose the
code whose API documentation will be extracted. These paths can lead
to both Scheme files or Scheme directories.

@defline {Keyword argument} --destination or -d
Optional path to a directory where the final
Texinfo files will be saved. If not provided, @var{DEFAULT_DOC_PATH}
is used (see the module @ref{documenta_commands}).
@end defblock
@c END CHAPTER: CLI



